{"authored" [{"question" "q-78680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/assets/14d1fc5a92c28cc7f84c1ec23929701f1aca67b1a15d6113fa83f1428354fc11.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(3,-1)$ with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $3,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,-1)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "9b9dd6614890663c50e9b3ef36c2f6a7c3e29b557686b711f27370ea081c787e" "basis" 1220 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,-1),$ $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "9b9dd6614890663c50e9b3ef36c2f6a7c3e29b557686b711f27370ea081c787e" "basis" 1220 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd2e5e871b6c74e0b963a0c4153f5795d89d5cab6bb61fa8d50bed50b80946ba" "basis" 1220 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "9b9dd6614890663c50e9b3ef36c2f6a7c3e29b557686b711f27370ea081c787e" "basis" 1220 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/14d1fc5a92c28cc7f84c1ec23929701f1aca67b1a15d6113fa83f1428354fc11.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(3,-1)$ with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $3,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,-1)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ce75818c43de0562e34ace87a7aa00ead016b7d5ae87d84477ab0fb4f0a47e" "basis" 1473 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,-1),$ $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ce75818c43de0562e34ace87a7aa00ead016b7d5ae87d84477ab0fb4f0a47e" "basis" 1473 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0f33df654564f4f131a84890341dea87fc6ce195c7505a09c3b16d77077ff5dc" "basis" 1473 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ce75818c43de0562e34ace87a7aa00ead016b7d5ae87d84477ab0fb4f0a47e" "basis" 1473 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-6881" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ce75818c43de0562e34ace87a7aa00ead016b7d5ae87d84477ab0fb4f0a47e" "basis" 1473 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-6881" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ce75818c43de0562e34ace87a7aa00ead016b7d5ae87d84477ab0fb4f0a47e" "basis" 1473 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-6881" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0f33df654564f4f131a84890341dea87fc6ce195c7505a09c3b16d77077ff5dc" "basis" 1473 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-6881" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ce75818c43de0562e34ace87a7aa00ead016b7d5ae87d84477ab0fb4f0a47e" "basis" 1473 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-6881" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-6881" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-6881" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2635445b150913bcd53486b6d971689d0bdcf679123dc7c31c1b1172fdd555e5"} {"question" "q-6881" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-6881" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-6881" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/assets/14d1fc5a92c28cc7f84c1ec23929701f1aca67b1a15d6113fa83f1428354fc11.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(3,-1)$ with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $3,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,-1)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,-1),$ $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2635445b150913bcd53486b6d971689d0bdcf679123dc7c31c1b1172fdd555e5"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1"] ["math" "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1"] ["math" "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1"] ["math" "x=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1"] ["math" "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-79273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/assets/48c5190fc4f45f4bf3e76a56977339559ceb30ca90a41411d3bc4df2362b0787.png)\n\nThe semi-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-79273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/assets/546b9f72124a7eb05877db3600e40b5772404a1a3378de67cfbe3320c78ac2ba.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-79273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2635445b150913bcd53486b6d971689d0bdcf679123dc7c31c1b1172fdd555e5"} {"question" "q-79273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-79273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-79273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-79287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/assets/07ec13485a828c8f5cd99ae2fbc3f7444135ba3c1cdf790762edb01b9726317e.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-79287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,2),$ $a=3$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-79287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2635445b150913bcd53486b6d971689d0bdcf679123dc7c31c1b1172fdd555e5"} {"question" "q-79287" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-79287" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3"] ["math" "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2"] ["math" "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2"] ["math" "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2"] ["math" "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-79287" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-79377" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-79377" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5 \\\\ \\frac{x+5}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2 \\\\ \\frac{y+2}{6} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-79377" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2635445b150913bcd53486b6d971689d0bdcf679123dc7c31c1b1172fdd555e5"} {"question" "q-79377" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1104d58fc793d489cfe3fd591794d39c77b482ec08addc04206095013de346d"} {"question" "q-79377" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{18}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{6}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{36}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/